/-!
# Local equations of a prime divisor on a regular scheme

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tags 0AGA, 0AG8, 0AG9 and 0AGB, together with
More on Algebra, Tag 0AG0 (blueprint: lem-per-regular-prime-divisor-local-equations).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme

/-- A prime divisor on an integral regular locally Noetherian scheme is effective Cartier.
Every generator of its stalk ideal is prime, and the canonical generization map exhibits
its generic ambient stalk as the localization at that prime ideal. This generic stalk is
a DVR, and the image of every such generator generates its maximal ideal.
The stalk ideal at `i x` is the kernel of `i.stalkMap x`, since `i` is a closed immersion.
The explicit algebra structure in the localization clause is induced by `stalkSpecializes`;
thus this clause specifies the canonical map, not merely an abstract ring isomorphism.

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tags 0AGA, 0AG8, 0AG9 and 0AGB
(blueprint: lem-per-regular-prime-divisor-local-equations).

Proof:
1. Choose a Noetherian affine neighbourhood Spec R of i(x). Integrality makes R a domain.
   The divisor is defined there by a prime ideal J, whose generic point is i(xi). If q
   corresponds to i(x), the affine-stalk and tilde-stalk localization descriptions give
   A = R_q, the stalk ideal p = J R_q, and the generic ambient stalk R_J.
2. The canonical generization sends a/s to a/s. Localizing again at p identifies A_p
   with R_J: the forward map sends (a/s)/(b/t) to at/(sb), and the inverse sends a/b
   to (a/1)/(b/1). Denominators avoid J, and the fraction relation verifies both maps.
3. Since dim R_J = 1, p is nonzero and has height one: primes below p correspond to
   primes of A_p, preserving strict inclusions. The zero prime is least since A is a domain.
4. A is regular local. Choose a regular parameter system and apply
   lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-factorization with the empty selected sublist to
   obtain unique factorization. The height-one-prime theorem gives p = (f). Primality
   and nonzeroness of p imply f is nonzero, nonunit, and divides one factor of any product
   it divides.
5. Write f = a/s with a in J and s outside q. Choose finite generators a_j of J.
   The equalities a_j/1 = (b_j/t_j)(a/1) hold after multiplying by annihilators u_j
   outside q. Invert the product of the u_j t_j to obtain J = (a) on a neighbourhood.
   The nonzero a is a nonzerodivisor because the coordinate ring is a domain.
6. Repeat at every point of the divisor; outside it the ideal is generated by 1.
   This is precisely the effective Cartier ideal condition.
7. For any generator g of p the same proper nonzero prime ideal argument proves Prime g.
   Step 2 uses this same ideal (g), and its extension is the maximal ideal of R_J.
8. The generic ambient stalk is regular local of dimension one, hence is a DVR by
   lem-sc-regular-dim1-dvr. Step 7 says that the image of g is a uniformizer. This
   includes x = xi and requires no global Noetherianity or bound on the dimension. -/
theorem regular_prime_divisor_local_equations
    (X D : Scheme.{u}) [IsIntegral X] [IsIntegral D] [IsLocallyNoetherian X]
    (hRegular : IsRegularScheme X) (i : D ⟶ X) [IsClosedImmersion i]
    (ξ : D) (hξ : IsGenericPoint ξ Set.univ)
    (hDim : ringKrullDim (X.presheaf.stalk (i ξ)) = 1) :
    i.ker.IsInvertible ∧ IsDiscreteValuationRing (X.presheaf.stalk (i ξ)) ∧
      ∀ (x : D) (f : X.presheaf.stalk (i x)),
        Ideal.span {f} = RingHom.ker (i.stalkMap x).hom →
        Prime f ∧ ∃ h : i ξ ⤳ i x,
          @IsLocalization.AtPrime (X.presheaf.stalk (i x)) _
            (X.presheaf.stalk (i ξ)) _ (X.presheaf.stalkSpecializes h).hom.toAlgebra
            (RingHom.ker (i.stalkMap x).hom) (RingHom.ker_isPrime (i.stalkMap x).hom) ∧
          IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (X.presheaf.stalk (i ξ)) =
            Ideal.span {(X.presheaf.stalkSpecializes h).hom f} := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme
